digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates why robust phase estimation fails to achieve Heisenberg optimality and classical Cramér-Rao optimality for multi-parameter gate calibration when gate diagonals do not share an identical phase, and develops/evaluates a quantum metrology protocol using fewer than ten shallow noise-tolerant circuits to bypass this phase-matching constraint and improve two-qubit gate-parameter estimation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Across the retained and repaired conclusions, existing gate-calibration metrology is constrained by precision gaps, RPE phase-matching failures, realistic circuit/noise limitations, and the need for Cramér-Rao-guided co-design, motivating a shallow noise-tolerant protocol for two-qubit gate-parameter estimation."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The observed failure of RPE-based multiphase estimation to reach Heisenberg scaling and classical Cramér-Rao bounds is plausibly explained by a structural phase-relation requirement among diagonal elements."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) A phase-matching condition on gate diagonal phases is a plausible structural requirement for RPE-based multi-parameter phase estimation to jointly achieve the desired optimality properties."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) When realistic multi-parameter gate-calibration tasks have two-qubit gate diagonal elements that do not share the same phase, the phase-matching condition is generically violated and RPE-based calibration is limited."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) For quantum metrology strategies that already adhere to Heisenberg scaling, efficiency improvement should consider the numerical coefficients in the resource scaling."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) In the present metrology context, some strategies adhere to Heisenberg scaling while efficiency is also determined by numerical scaling coefficients."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) The Ref2 quantum-simulation example contrasts approximately 10^-2 rad practical errors with in-principle 10^-4 rad Fourier-transform uncertainty, indicating a precision gap in that specific setting."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) In gate calibration, existing protocols reach about 10^-2–10^-3 rad for gate-angle estimation whereas fault-tolerant threshold verification requires about 10^-4 rad, indicating a substantial application-specific precision gap."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) Under ideal and static conditions, RPE-type methods can in principle offer efficient and SPAM-robust calibration because RPE/Floquet calibration can achieve the Heisenberg limit and RPE-type methods are robust to SPAM errors."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) Randomized benchmarking typically involves long gate sequences and therefore presents an extensive-circuit-depth burden in practice."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) Realizing Cramér-Rao optimality in gate calibration requires refining both the classical inference subroutines and the quantum measurement or circuit designs."];
  "R33" [label="(0,0,0) Given existing protocols' struggles with realistic time-dependent errors and deep-circuit constraints, metrology theory motivates introducing a shallow, noise-robust, Cramér-Rao-oriented protocol."];
  "R34" [label="(0,0,0) The proposed protocol's shallow-circuit, time-dependent-error-robust, and high-precision design makes it a plausible candidate for addressing gate-calibration metrology limitations."];
  "R35" [label="(0,0,0) A protocol designed to be robust to realistic time-dependent errors, to operate with shallow circuits, and to improve gate-parameter precision directly addresses key practical inefficiencies in learning two-qubit gates."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) In the Ref2 quantum-simulation setting, practical approximately 10^-2 rad errors and in-principle 10^-4 rad Fourier-transform statistical uncertainty indicate a context-specific precision gap between demonstrated practice and theoretically meaningful resolution."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) If quantum metrology efficiency is fundamentally influenced by the Heisenberg scaling law and by the associated scaling coefficients, then any attempt to improve efficiency must consider both the scaling law and its coefficients."];
  "n103" [label="(0,0,0) If a metrology bottleneck such as non-optimal scaling coefficients limits gate characterization accuracy, and high-fidelity quantum error correction requires extremely accurate gate characterization, then resolving that metrology bottleneck is critical for achieving fault tolerance."];
  "n104" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that non-optimal scaling coefficients limit achievable accuracy and fault-tolerant QEC demands very high accuracy, therefore optimizing these coefficients is crucial for enabling fault-tolerant quantum error correction."];
  "n107" [label="(0,0,0) If the required accuracy to certify fault-tolerant thresholds is ~10^-4 rad but currently available protocols achieve only 10^-2–10^-3 rad, then these protocols cannot reliably certify threshold crossing for fault-tolerant QEC or similar applications."];
  "n117" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that Cramér–Rao bounds are fundamental limits and both classical and quantum parts are optimizable, therefore achieving Cramér–Rao optimality in practice necessitates co-design and refinement of both classical post-processing and quantum circuit or measurement design."];
  "n118" [label="(0,0,0) If in multiparameter phase estimation structural conditions on the target unitary such as phase relations among diagonal elements determine whether a given phase-estimation scheme can jointly achieve Heisenberg scaling and the classical Cramér–Rao bound, then a missing structural condition can explain systematic failures of such schemes."];
  "n119" [label="(0,0,0) Currently empirically or theoretically, RPE-based multiphase estimation fails to reach Heisenberg scaling and classical Cramér–Rao bounds when there are multiple phases to estimate, even without quantum noise."];
  "n120" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given background knowledge that structural conditions control joint optimality and the observed failure of RPE-based multiphase estimation, therefore the best explanation is that an additional phase-matching condition on diagonal elements is required."];
  "n121" [label="(0,0,0) If a known method such as RPE-based multi-parameter estimation fails to achieve Heisenberg scaling and classical Cramér–Rao bounds whenever a phase-matching condition is violated, then any protocol that relies on this method without ensuring phase matching cannot be fundamentally optimal in such multi-phase settings."];
  "n122" [label="(0,0,0) Currently in realistic multi-parameter gate-calibration tasks, diagonal elements of two-qubit gates need not share the same phase, so the phase-matching condition is generically violated."];
  "n123" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that RPE-based methods require phase matching and realistic gates generally violate it, therefore generic RPE-based multi-parameter calibration schemes are fundamentally limited and cannot simultaneously achieve Heisenberg scaling and classical Cramér–Rao optimality in typical two-qubit gate-learning tasks."];
  "n124" [label="(0,0,0) Currently we observe that existing metrology protocols struggle under realistic time-dependent errors and deep-circuit constraints, yet practical applications demand shallow-depth, drift-robust, high-precision gate calibration."];
  "n125" [label="(0,0,0) If metrology and QEC theory indicate that a protocol intentionally designed to be noise-robust especially to slow drifts, to use shallow circuits, and to implement Cramér–Rao-oriented classical and quantum optimization could close the precision gap, then such a protocol is a plausible solution to current limitations."];
  "n128" [label="(0,0,0) Currently the protocol proposed in this work is explicitly described as robust to realistic time-dependent errors, using shallow (<10) circuits, and yielding up to two orders of magnitude improvement in gate-parameter precision over existing methods."];
  "n17" [label="(6,1,0) If current protocols achieve only 10^-2–10^-3 rad precision for gate-angle estimation but ~10^-4 rad is required to verify crossing the fault-tolerant threshold, then current protocols are insufficient for verifying fault tolerance in near-term applications."];
  "n18" [label="(6,1,0) Currently quantum metrology protocols achieve only 10^-2–10^-3 rad accuracy for estimating gate angles."];
  "n19" [label="(6,2,0) Currently about 10^-4 rad accuracy is needed to verify that fault-tolerant error thresholds have been crossed for QEC and other near-term applications."];
  "n22" [label="(8,1,0) Currently RPE and Floquet calibration can achieve the Heisenberg limit under ideal conditions."];
  "n23" [label="(8,2,0) Currently RPE-type methods are robust against SPAM errors for both single- and multi-qubit gates."];
  "n28" [label="(13,1,0) Currently randomized benchmarking also requires extensive circuit depth for accurate parameter estimation and lacks sensitivity to coherent rotation errors."];
  "n33" [label="(16,1,0) Currently to meet the classical Cramér–Rao bound, the classical inference subroutines that process measurement data into gate-parameter estimates must be optimal."];
  "n39" [label="(7,7,8) Currently according to prior work, randomized benchmarking typically involves long gate sequences (deep circuits) when applied in practice."];
  "n42" [label="(2,2,7) Currently according to Ref2, accurate quantum simulation of condensed-matter systems can reach ~10^-2 rad errors in energy eigenvalue estimation at physical energy scales ~1 rad, illustrating a practical precision regime around 10^-2 rad."];
  "n43" [label="(2,2,10) Currently according to Ref2, Fourier-transform based techniques can in principle resolve eigenenergies with statistical uncertainty of 10^-4 rad, indicating that 10^-4 rad precision is physically meaningful and technologically relevant."];
  "n5" [label="(2,1,0) Currently a variety of quantum metrology strategies successfully adhere to Heisenberg scaling."];
  "n7" [label="(3,1,0) If optimality in scaling coefficients is vital for quantum error correction because fault-tolerant thresholds demand very high gate-characterization accuracy, then insufficient scaling coefficients will obstruct achieving fault tolerance."];
  "n8" [label="(3,1,0) Currently optimality in scaling coefficients is particularly vital for quantum error correction applications."];
  "n9" [label="(3,2,0) Currently achieving fault-tolerant thresholds in quantum error correction requires exceptionally high accuracy in quantum gate characterization."];
  "n7" -> "n103" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n9" -> "n103" [label="deduction-case"];
